# Round 72 — zeros of derivatives and a-points

| item | proven | link to ζ zeros | verdict |
|------|--------|-----------------|---------|
| Speiser / Levinson–Montgomery | ζ off-line zero ⇔ ζ' zero in 0 < σ < 1/2 | equivalence | CIRCULAR |
| ζ' zeros near the line (Soundararajan, Zhang, Ki) | small gaps ⇒ ζ' zeros near the line | proximity only | DEAD |
| Ξ^(k) for large k (Craven–Csordas–Smith, Ki–Kim) | real-rooted for all large k | derivatives keep real roots; anti-derivatives do not | DEAD |
| a-points (Levinson 1975) | density one near σ = 1/2 for a ≠ 0 | a = 0 excluded | DEAD |
| Conrey 1983 | proportion of Ξ^(k) zeros on the line → 1 | not ζ zeros | DEAD |
| Levinson's V(s) | counting device | auxiliary zeros | DEAD |
| Ki–Kim–Lee | Ξ^(k) real-rooted, large k | as above | DEAD |
| Hardy Z', Gram's law | Rolle; Gram's law fails | extrema, not zeros | DEAD |

Differentiation pushes zeros toward the line (Laguerre–Pólya is closed under derivatives), but going back
from Ξ^(k) to Ξ is integration, which can create non-real zeros — the hard direction again.
